          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Measurable spaces are the traditional prelude to the general theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure"} measure
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration"} integration
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsets"} subsets
            of a given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            for which the size is well-defined; these subsets are called ‘measurable’. The measure on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is then an operation that assigns a number to each measurable subset saying how big it is.
          %p
            In short: you get a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+space"} measure space
            by placing a measure on a measurable space.
          %p
            Ideally, all subsets would be measurable, but this contradicts the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            for the basic example of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lebesgue+measure"} Lebesgue measure
            on the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundations
            of mathematics in which all subsets of the real line are Lebesgue measurable, any general theory that includes that example and is more general than those foundations requires some explicit notion of measurable space (or an alternative such as a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+locale"} measurable locale
          %p In any case, measurable spaces are of some interest in their own right, even without a measure on them.
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p
            We give first the usual notion, assuming the validity of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            and
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+sets"} power sets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicative+mathematics"} predicative
            theories.
          %p
            Given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %strong
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
              \-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-algebra"} algebra
            is a collection of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsets"} subsets
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            that is closed under
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complement"} complementation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+set"} countable
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unions"} unions
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersections"} intersections
            = succeed "," do
              %strong measurable space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            equipped with a
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            are called the
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+sets"} measurable sets
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (or more properly, the measurable subsets of
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\Sigma)
          %p
            Given measurable spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+function"} measurable function
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon X \to Y
            such that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preimage"} preimage
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*(T)
            is measurable in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            whenever
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is measurable in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Meas"} Meas
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+concrete+category"} topological
            over
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
          %p
            In classical measure theory, it is usually assumed that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
            (or a variation) equipped with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borel+sets"} Borel sets
            (see the examples below). Then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is measurable if and only if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{-1}(I)
            is measurable whenever
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \subseteq Y
            is an interval.
          %h2#variations Variations
          %p We will briefly examine variations of the notion of measurable space, from those most like the standard to those most unlike it. Most of these are discussed at articles dedicated to them.
          %p
            Historically, people have used more general notions that
            = succeed "-algebras," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            = succeed "-rings," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-algebra"} sigma-algebra
            = succeed "-algebras," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+functions"} partial functions
            whose domain is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relatively+measurable+set"} relatively measurable set
            as measurable functions; for details, see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+function"} measurable function
          %p
            An
            %strong enhanced measurable space
            has, in addition to the
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-ideal"} ideal
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                measurable
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/null+sets"} null sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and the
            = succeed "-alebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N \subseteq \Sigma
            that is closed under
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+set"} countable
            unions and taking subsets (within
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            are the
            %em measurable
            null sets; a
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/null+set"} null set
            is
            %em any
            subset of a measurable null set.) One can equivalently specify a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delta-filter"} filter
            of
            = succeed ";" do
              %strong
                measurable
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+sets"} full sets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complements"} complements
            of the null sets. Either way, this allows us to use almost measurable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/almost+functions"} almost functions
            up to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/almost+equality"} almost equality
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+function"} measurable function
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complement"} complementation
            doesn't behave nicely, the concept of
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/almost+functions"} almost functions
            to avoid a paucity of measurable functions. One solution, due to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Henry+Cheng"} Henry Cheng
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cheng+measurable+space"} Cheng measurable space
            %span.newWikiWord
              disjoint pairs
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/disjoint+pairs"} ?
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A,B)
            of sets instead of individual measurable sets and use formal complements in the algebra, as well as a notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+sets"} full sets
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
          %p
            In order to have the most important theorems of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+theory"} measure theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localizable+measures"} localizable measures
            = succeed ":" do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localizable+measurable+space"} localizable measurable space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boolean+algebra"} boolean algebra
            of measurable sets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+algebra"} modulo
            the null sets is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+lattice"} complete
          %p
            Another approach to measure theory, more abstract, is to ignore the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and use
            %em only
            the
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boolean+algebra"} boolean algebra
            equipped with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+set"} countable
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/suprema"} suprema
            %strong
              %span.newWikiWord
                measurable algebra
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/measurable+algebra"} ?
            (or a
            %span.newWikiWord
              measure algebra
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/measure+algebra"} ?
            when equipped with a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure"} measure
            = succeed "-ideal" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            = succeed "-filter" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+algebra"} quotient algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+lattice"} complete lattice
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+measure"} normal measure
            = succeed ";" do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+locale"} measurable locale
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of measurable locales is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalent
            to that of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localizable+measurable+spaces"} localizable measurable spaces
            (from the previous paragraph).
          %p
            Yet another category equivalent to localizable measurable spaces and measurable locales is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
            of the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/von+Neumann+algebras"} von Neumann algebras
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gelfand%E2%80%93Neumark+theorem"} Gelfand–Neumark theorem
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+geometry"} noncommutative
            (localizable) measurable space is (the formal dual of)
            %em any
            von Neumann algebra. In this way, measure theory may be seen as a branch of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator+algebra"} operator algebra
            theory (at least if one assumes that only localizable measurable spaces are well enough behaved to be worthy of study).
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Of course, the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+set"} power set
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is closed under all operations, so it is a
                = succeed "-algebra." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi σ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
                = succeed "." do
                  %strong
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+space"} discrete
                    measurable space
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
                = succeed "-algebra" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi σ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is the
                = succeed ";" do
                  %strong
                    Borel
                    = succeed "-algebra" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi σ
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borel+sets"} Borel sets
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real number
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
                with its usual topology.
            %li
              %p
                If a measurable space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\Sigma)
                is equipped with a measure
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi μ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+space"} measure space
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi σ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-ideal"} ideal
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
                (that is an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ideal"} ideal
                that is also a sub-
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
                \-ring). Let a
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/null+subset"} null set
                be
                %em any
                set (measurable or not) contained in a set of measure zero; then the null sets form a
                = succeed "-ideal" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi σ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+set"} power set
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %strong
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi μ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
                  \-measurable
                if it is the union of a measurable set and a null set; then the
                = succeed "-measurable" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi μ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
                = succeed "-algebra" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi σ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mi μ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_\mu
                called the
                %strong completion
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
                under
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi μ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mi μ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\Sigma_\mu)
                is the
                %strong completion
                of
                = succeed "." do
